    proTip: 'Roast the beans alone for the first 4–5 minutes, then slide the salmon on. Salmon and green beans look like they should cook in the same window, but the beans are naked vegetable and the salmon is a dense protein — starting the beans first is how you get char on the beans instead of grey, limp beans huddled around perfect fish.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS Safe Minimum Internal Temperatures chart lists 145°F for fin fish. The 12–14 minute window at 400°F is standard center-cut-fillet convection roasting practice for a 1–1.25 inch fillet; thinner tail pieces will finish 2–3 minutes sooner and should be pulled early rather than averaged.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Salmon\'s flakes are bound by thin collagen sheets called myocommata that begin converting to gelatin below the temperature at which the muscle fibers themselves fully coagulate, which is why a properly cooked fillet separates cleanly instead of shredding. Pulling it at 145°F catches the interior just as the surface albumin, a more heat-sensitive protein, starts pushing out — a sign the flesh has cleared the USDA threshold without the muscle fibers contracting hard enough to squeeze out moisture and turn chalky. Green beans have no protein to denature; their doneness comes from pectin softening in the pod walls and surface sugars caramelizing wherever a bean touches the hot metal.',
