    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Pull the thighs to 175°F, not 165°F. Dark meat gets its silky texture from collagen breakdown, which does not really start until the 170s — a thigh probed at exactly 165°F is technically safe but reads as rubbery and tight. The extra 10°F is the difference between "safe" and "the meat you actually want".',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS Safe Minimum Internal Temperatures chart lists 165°F for poultry; the 175°F pull cited here exceeds that minimum for texture reasons, so the safety floor is comfortably met. Standard convection roasting window at 400°F for bone-in thighs weighing 5–7 oz each.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Bone conducts heat more slowly than the surrounding muscle, so meat near the thigh-drumstick joint lags behind the rest of the piece — which is exactly why the target is read there rather than at the thickest part of the flesh; if the joint clears the mark, everything else already has. Skin-side up on a thin sheet pan lets rendered fat drip straight down through the meat and onto the vegetables, basting the potatoes and carrots in schmaltz while radiant heat from above crisps the skin. Parsnips carry more natural sugar than potatoes or carrots, so at the same 400°F they caramelize fastest and are usually the first vegetable done.',
