    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS lowered the safe pork minimum to 145°F with a 3-minute rest in 2011 (from 160°F previously); this datasheet reflects the current standard. Cook time is calibrated to 1-inch bone-in chops at 400°F — thinner chops finish 4–5 minutes sooner.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Pork muscle denatures in two stages: myosin sets first, in a range that still reads juicy, while actin — the protein responsible for dry, tough texture — doesn\'t fully contract until well above 145°F. Pulling the chop at that number, with a three-minute carryover rest, catches the meat after myosin sets but before actin squeezes out the bulk of its moisture. The rib bone sits closer to one face of the chop than the other, acting as a local heat shield, so meat right against it can lag slightly behind a probe reading taken elsewhere. Onion has essentially no protein, so its browning here is straight sugar caramelization rather than a Maillard reaction.',
