    technicalDeepDive: 'A cut sprout face exposes rows of ruptured plant cells directly to the pan, giving sugars and amino acids a flat, high-contact surface to Maillard-brown against, while the still-intact outer leaves rely on drier convective heat and crisp more like kale chips than like the seared cut face. Rendering bacon fat matters beyond flavor: fat transfers heat more effectively than the air pockets between sprout halves, so wherever it pools and coats a cut surface it speeds browning. Brassica vegetables carry sulfur compounds that read as bitter when raw or lightly steamed, and sustained heat at 425°F breaks many of those down while building sweetness through caramelization, which is why a well-roasted sprout tastes so different from a boiled one.',
