    donenessCue: 'Floret tops have gone deep brown with a few nearly-black tips, cut stem faces are caramelized where they touched the pan, and a fork slides into the thickest stem with no crunch.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Cut the florets so the flat side of the stem sits directly on the pan, not stem-up. The caramelization that makes roasted broccoli worth eating happens where vegetable meets metal — a floret balanced on its bushy top is a floret that steams instead of browns.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — broccoli florets are judged by cut-face caramelization and fork-tender stem, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 425°F/18–22 minute window reflects standard high-heat convection roasting for brassica florets in a single spaced layer; a lower temperature causes them to weep and turn olive-drab before browning.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Broccoli is mostly water trapped inside pectin-rich cell walls, so the first several minutes at 425°F go almost entirely into driving that surface moisture off rather than building color — nothing can brown while water is still evaporating and holding the surface near the boiling point. Flipping at the twelve-minute mark exposes the side that spent the first half of cooking shielded from direct pan contact, letting it dry out and pick up its own browning before the floret finishes. The branching, irregular shape of a broccoli head also means individual florets vary a lot in mass, which is why the smallest pieces develop nearly-black tips well before the thickest stems turn fork-tender.',
    keywords: ['how long to roast broccoli', 'sheet pan broccoli time', 'roasted broccoli temperature', 'crispy roasted broccoli', 'broccoli oven 425'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to roast broccoli florets on a sheet pan: 425°F (218°C) for 18–22 minutes. Why the flat side has to face the pan.',
