    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS lists no minimum internal temperature for baked potatoes (they are not a food-safety category), but the practical doneness target is 205°F: this is where russet starch granules have fully gelatinized and the cell walls have collapsed enough to produce the fluffy interior texture. Cook time is calibrated to 10–12 oz russets at 400°F on a bare rack or sheet pan; smaller potatoes finish 15 minutes sooner and jumbo bakers add 15–20 minutes.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A whole 10-12 oz russet is dense enough that heat reaches the center only through slow conduction inward from the skin, which is why the bake runs past an hour even at 400°F — for food this size and this low in fat, the potato\'s own internal moisture has to carry that heat toward the core rather than fat or thin geometry doing the work. Pricking the skin gives that moisture an escape route once it turns to steam; without vents, trapped vapor pressure can split the skin outright. Sitting directly on bare metal rather than a rack also means the bottom of each potato picks up extra conductive heat beyond what the other exposed sides get from convection alone.',
