    donenessCue: 'Cut faces are deeply browned with charred edges and lacy dark tips on the outer florets; the stem end of the biggest floret gives without resistance to a paring knife; the smallest crumbs that fell off during tossing have turned into crunchy salty chips.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Use more oil than feels right — cauliflower is thirsty. Three tablespoons for a head sounds like a lot, but under-oiled cauliflower goes dusty and grey before it browns; well-oiled cauliflower turns mahogany and picks up a nutty, almost-popcorn flavor from the caramelizing surface sugars.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — cauliflower is judged by cut-face caramelization and paring-knife-tender stem, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 425°F/22–28 minute window reflects standard high-heat convection roasting for medium florets in a single spaced layer; a lower temperature or a crowded pan produces steamed, pale cauliflower before browning has a chance to start.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Cauliflower\'s curd is a dense cluster of tightly packed floret buds rather than the open, branching habit of a leafier brassica, so an equivalent-sized piece carries more mass relative to its exposed surface and needs longer for heat to work through the core — part of why this roast runs several minutes past what a looser vegetable needs at the same 425°F. That density also means individual florets hold internal moisture longer than their surface alone suggests, so real caramelization only gets going once enough of that moisture has migrated out past the cut faces. The smallest loose crumbs that fall off during tossing carry almost no mass at all, so they turn into dark, crunchy chips well before the larger florets have caught up.',
    keywords: ['how long to roast cauliflower', 'sheet pan cauliflower time', 'roasted cauliflower temperature', 'crispy cauliflower florets', 'cauliflower oven 425'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to roast cauliflower florets on a sheet pan: 425°F (218°C) for 22–28 minutes. Why cauliflower needs more oil than you think.',
