    restMinutes: 3,
    donenessCue: 'Pepper edges are blistered black in spots, onions have gone translucent with charred tips, and steak strips have deep-brown crust on the outside with visible pink pull-back at the cut edges — probe a thick strip and pull when it reads 130–145°F depending on preferred doneness.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Slice against the grain BEFORE cooking, not after. Skirt and flank have long, coarse muscle fibers that read as chewy no matter how well you cooked them — slicing across those fibers into 1/2-inch strips shortens every fiber so each bite is tender. Slicing after cooking is fine for a whole steak; for fajitas the pre-slice is what makes them work at high heat.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS Safe Minimum Internal Temperatures chart lists 145°F for beef whole muscle (steaks, roasts) with a 3-minute rest. Skirt and flank strips reach 130°F medium-rare in 7–9 minutes at 450°F and 145°F medium in 10–14 minutes; the safety floor is comfortably met at either pull if the meat is not ground and comes from an intact whole muscle.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A half-inch strip carries very little mass between its outer crust and geometric center, so there\'s only a narrow window before conduction carries the interior past medium-rare — 450°F is what lets a real Maillard crust form in that window instead of the surface merely drying while the core slowly catches up. Flipping at the seven-minute mark exposes the second face to the same direct pan contact the first already had, which matters more for a thin strip than a thick steak since so little total cook time is available for either side to brown. Peppers and onions sharing the pan blister and char at that same aggressive heat because their thin cell walls let internal moisture flash to steam and escape almost immediately, rupturing the skin from within.',
