    verificationBasis: 'Camp Chef Woodwind Pro published turkey-leg cooking guide (275°F to 175°F+ for dark-meat rendering); USDA FSIS 165°F all-poultry safe minimum; USDA FSIS guidance on Prague Powder #1 use in home cured meats (150 ppm nitrite maximum for immersion brines).',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Turkey drumstick muscle is threaded with thick tendons built largely from collagen, and that connective tissue needs sustained heat well above the USDA\'s 165°F poultry minimum before it hydrolyzes into gelatin, which is why this cut is pulled so much later than a boneless breast would be. The wet brine\'s dissolved salt denatures surface proteins and lets the fibers retain more of their own moisture, offsetting the drying effect of a multi-hour 275°F cook. The curing salt\'s nitrite also penetrates deep into the muscle and bonds to myoglobin there, fixing a stable pink pigment that survives cooking and is chemically distinct from the thin nitric-oxide smoke ring that forms only at the surface.',
    keywords: ['how long to smoke turkey legs', 'smoked turkey legs time', 'smoker turkey legs temp', 'disney turkey leg smoker', 'smoked turkey drumsticks'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook turkey legs in the smoker: 275°F (135°C) for 2.5–3 hours. Internal target 175°F–185°F (dark-meat tender). Verified times, no guesswork.',
