    proTip: 'Thighs are the exception to the 165°F rule — connective tissue only breaks down around 175°F, so a thigh pulled at the safe minimum is safe but still gummy. Aim for 175°F if you want it to shred instead of chunk.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart (165°F poultry minimum, connective-tissue breakdown at ~175°F for dark meat); poaching technique corroborated by ThermoWorks poached chicken guide (185°F bath temperature).',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Thigh meat carries noticeably more intramuscular fat and myoglobin than breast, both of which continue basting the muscle fibers from inside as the poach runs, part of why thigh tolerates a longer cook without drying out the way lean breast meat would over the same stretch. Holding the bath at a gentle 185°F instead of a rolling boil also matters mechanically: aggressive bubbling agitates the meat and forces moisture out through sheer turbulence on top of whatever the proteins lose by contracting on their own, so the gentle simmer controls agitation as much as temperature. Boneless thigh is also small and uniform enough that fifteen to eighteen minutes gives that gentler heat time to reach every fiber evenly without needing a hotter bath to get there.',
    keywords: ['how long to poach chicken thighs', 'boiling chicken thighs', 'poached chicken thighs time', 'shredded chicken thigh method', 'chicken thighs internal temp shred'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to poach boneless chicken thighs: 185°F (85°C) gentle simmer for 15–18 min to 175°F (shreds cleanly). Verified against USDA FSIS.',
