    restMinutes: 60,
    donenessCue: 'Crust is deep mahogany, not tan; the loaf sounds hollow tapped on the base; centre reads 205°F.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'The lid is the whole trick — it traps the dough’s own steam for the first 30 minutes, which is what gives the oven spring and the blistered crust. Taking the lid off early is the single most common cause of a dense, pale loaf. Resist cutting for a full hour: the crumb is still setting and a hot loaf cuts gummy.',
    verificationBasis: 'King Arthur Baking no-knead method (preheated covered Dutch oven, 450°F, 30 min covered then uncovered to colour); 205–210°F is the standard finish range for lean hearth dough.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A lean dough carries no fat to slow surface moisture loss, so trapping its own steam under the lid holds the crust near boiling long enough for full oven spring before the uncovered stage lets that surface finally climb into the 280-330°F Maillard band that turns it mahogany. The crumb underneath never approaches that range; it only needs to reach 205-210°F to fully gelatinize the starch and lock in the gluten network that hours of fermentation built without any kneading. Cast iron\'s mass conducts heat steadily from the pot wall straight into the dough rather than relying on the surrounding air, which is why preheating the empty pot matters nearly as much as the oven temperature itself.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'dutch-oven-no-knead-bread',
    keywords: ['how long to bake no knead bread in dutch oven', 'dutch oven bread temperature', 'no knead bread internal temp', 'dutch oven bread 450', 'how long to bake bread in a dutch oven'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to bake no-knead bread in a Dutch oven: 450°F, 30 minutes covered then 13–18 uncovered, to 205°F internal. Verified method, no fluff.',
