    technicalDeepDive: 'Onions carry a lot of water relative to their sugar content, and that water has to evaporate before caramelization can meaningfully begin, which is why the first stretch of cooking shows almost no color change: surface temperature stays pinned near 212°F by the energy cost of vaporizing that moisture, the same latent-heat stall that keeps a wet steak from searing. Only once enough moisture is gone does direct conduction from the pot\'s metal, pressed against onions already collapsed from the initial covered sweat, let surface temperature climb high enough for the onions\' own sugars to caramelize. At the same time those same reducing sugars reacting with free amino acids drive a separate Maillard reaction, and it\'s the two reactions layering together that build the depth a slow caramelization is chasing.',
