    technicalDeepDive: 'Loin meat is lean with almost no intramuscular fat, so it has little buffer against moisture loss once its muscle fibers pass through their coagulation range, which is why the safe target window here is only a few degrees wide. Bone conducts heat more slowly than surrounding muscle and acts as a partial insulator, so meat right against it lags a few degrees behind the rest of the chop; the indirect 350°F phase gives that lagging area time to catch up by convection after the 500°F direct sear has already set the crust. Searing first, rather than after, matters on a chop this thick because a crust built while the core is still cold survives the indirect finish without the interior overshooting 145°F.',
