    technicalDeepDive: 'Buttermilk\'s acidity reacts with the baking soda the instant it\'s mixed in, so carbon dioxide starts forming throughout the batter before the pancake ever touches the pan; what happens on the skillet is mostly about capturing that gas rather than creating it. As the batter\'s edges hit the hot surface, egg and wheat proteins begin coagulating from the outside in while starch granules absorb water and swell, and that outside-in progression is what lets bubbles rising from deeper in the batter still find a path through a surface that hasn\'t fully set. The open holes left behind are simply craters where trapped CO2 broke through that half-set surface and escaped rather than staying suspended in the crumb.',
