    technicalDeepDive: 'Pounding the breast to a uniform half-inch thickness controls how conductive heat travels through the meat: chicken breast is lean, dense muscle with almost no intramuscular fat to buffer against overcooking, so thickness variation leaves thin sections dry before the thick lobe catches up. At a uniform thickness, heat from the pan reaches the geometric center at nearly the same rate across the whole cutlet, which is why pulling at 160°F leaves just enough residual heat to finish denaturing proteins during the rest without pushing the thin edges past their moisture-holding capacity. Actin, largely responsible for squeezing moisture out as chicken overcooks, denatures at a noticeably higher temperature than myosin.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'skillet-marry-me-chicken',
    keywords: ['how long to cook chicken breast in skillet', 'pan seared chicken breast time', 'skillet chicken breast internal temp', 'how to sear chicken breast without drying', 'stove top chicken breast cook time'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook boneless chicken breast in a skillet: 8–10 min at medium-high. Why pounding to 1/2-inch thickness prevents rubbery dry meat.',
