    technicalDeepDive: 'Bone conducts heat more slowly than the surrounding muscle, so meat nearest the bone lags slightly behind the rest of the chop, buying a small margin against overcooking while the broader muscle races ahead on direct pan conduction. Modern pork is bred lean, with little of the intramuscular fat that once cushioned older breeds against drying out, so pulling at 140°F and letting carryover finish the rise to 145°F keeps the fibers just past the coagulation point instead of well past it. Basting with hot butter gives the top surface, which never touches the pan directly, a second heat path so it browns nearly as fast as the side in direct metal contact.',
