    technicalDeepDive: 'An acidic marinade shifts the pH at the meat\'s surface enough to unfold some surface proteins before the steak ever meets heat, part of why marinated flank feels less resistant at the bite even before the grill does anything. That same marinade leaves the surface wetter than a dry-brined steak, so blotting it fully dry is what lets a 550°F grate drive the surface into the Maillard range quickly instead of stalling near the boiling point while residual moisture evaporates. Flank is a long, single-direction muscle with comparatively little internal fat, so at only about 3/4 inch at its thickest it moves through its coagulation window fast — direct radiant heat with a single flip builds a full crust before the core passes 130°F.',
