    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 165°F minimum internal temperature for all poultry parts; 175–195°F dark-meat pull range per Meathead Goldwyn\'s poultry temperature guide on AmazingRibs.com and the Weber Grilling Guide\'s bone-in chicken method.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The drumstick\'s meat wraps a single long bone that is denser and slower to heat than the flesh around it, so muscle nearest the bone always lags the surface by several degrees — part of why the 375°F indirect stage runs long enough for that gap to close before the direct sear touches the skin. Leg muscle does more mechanical work in a live bird than wing muscle does, so drumsticks carry more collagen through the meat itself, not just at the joints; that collagen needs sustained heat well above the 165°F safety floor to convert fully to gelatin, the real reason the pull range extends toward 195°F. The longer 8-to-24-hour dry brine reflects the greater mass here compared to a flat, thin wing.',
