    donenessCue: 'A hard, evenly-dark bark from the sear on all faces, and a probe slid into the thickest end of the roast — the fatter tri-tip end, not the flat tail — reads 130°F.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'The grain of a tri-tip changes direction: the fibres in the thick end run at a roughly 45° angle to the fibres in the tail. Cut the roast in half through the middle first, then slice each half against its own grain — one cutting board direction leaves half the roast chewy no matter how well you cooked it.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F safe minimum for whole beef; 130°F medium-rare pull per Serious Eats\' reverse-sear tri-tip testing (Kenji López-Alt) and the Weber Grilling Guide indirect-then-sear method. Two-grain slicing guidance from Meathead Goldwyn\'s tri-tip primer on AmazingRibs.com.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Starting tri-tip at a moderate 275°F indirect heat rather than searing first lets the roast\'s core rise slowly and evenly, because a large gap between a hot grate and a cold interior always overcooks a wide outer band before the center catches up — the low, steady heat here nearly eliminates that gray ring. Pulling at 115°F and finishing over 550°F direct heat inverts the usual order for a reason: the surface is already dry from the slow indirect phase, so it needs only a brief blast to cross into the Maillard range and build a crust, rather than the longer direct exposure a raw-surfaced roast would require and that would keep driving heat into an already-warm core past medium-rare. Because tri-tip is a roast of uneven thickness, this method also gives the thinner tail more forgiving margin than a straight hard sear would.',
    keywords: ['how long to grill tri tip', 'tri tip grill time', 'tri tip internal temperature', 'reverse sear tri tip', 'grilled tri tip medium rare', 'tri tip grain direction'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to grill tri-tip reverse-sear: 275°F indirect to 115°F, then 550°F sear to a 130°F final. Why tri-tip has two grains and how to slice it.',
