    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Buy thick spears specifically for the grill — pencil-thin asparagus scorches to black husks in the time it takes the thick middles to warm through, and a mixed bunch cooks to two different doneness levels no matter what you do. Snap the woody ends off with your hands rather than measuring a cut point; each spear breaks naturally at the tender/tough boundary.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — asparagus is judged by the bend test and colour, so internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2, matching the corn and mushroom conventions in this dataset. Direct-heat method and thick-spear guidance from the Weber Grilling Guide grilled-vegetables chart and Serious Eats\' grilled-asparagus testing.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Asparagus has no muscle protein to denature — its texture is governed by pectin, the cement between plant cell walls, which needs sustained heat to break down enough for a spear to bend instead of snapping. At 450°F direct heat, the outer surface reaches caramelization and light Maillard territory fast enough to blister and char before the pectin deep inside has fully softened, which is part of why constant rolling matters: it spreads that surface heat evenly rather than committing one face to full char while the far side stays raw-crisp. The bright green color survives because the exposure is short — chlorophyll only degrades into the duller olive tones of pheophytin with far longer heat exposure than a five-to-eight-minute grill session provides.',
    keywords: ['how long to grill asparagus', 'grilled asparagus time', 'asparagus on the grill', 'grilled asparagus temperature', 'how to grill asparagus spears'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to grill asparagus: 450°F direct, 5–8 min with a quarter-turn every 90 sec. Why thick spears beat pencil-thin every time.',
