    proTip: 'Reverse-sear is the only method for a 2-inch bone-in — a straight direct grill either scorches the outer inch black by the time the centre reaches medium-rare, or produces a grey ring 3/4 of the way in. The slow indirect leg dries the surface for a better crust and warms the interior evenly; the direct finish then only needs a minute or two per face because it is building colour, not cooking the middle.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F safe minimum for whole beef cuts; 125°F pull for medium-rare and reverse-sear temperature ladder from Meathead Goldwyn\'s reverse-sear guide on AmazingRibs.com and J. Kenji López-Alt\'s reverse-sear steak testing (Serious Eats). Weber Grilling Guide thick bone-in ribeye chart used for the indirect/direct time split.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A slab this thick behaves like two problems stacked together: the crust needs a very high surface temperature to hit the Maillard range, while the core only needs to arrive gently at 115°F, and no single heat level does both well. The 275°F indirect stage handles the core through slow, even conduction, giving collagen and fat time above room temperature to loosen and begin rendering before any crust forms. Because the long uncovered dry brine has already pulled surface moisture into solution and let it evaporate, the meat meeting the 550°F sear has almost no free water left to absorb as latent heat, so the crust climbs into Maillard range within minutes instead of stalling near the boiling point of surface moisture.',
    keywords: ['how long to grill cowboy ribeye', 'bone in ribeye grill time', 'reverse sear ribeye grill', 'cowboy steak internal temperature', 'thick ribeye grill'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to grill a 2-inch cowboy ribeye: reverse sear at 275°F indirect to 115°F, then 550°F direct to a 125°F pull. Why direct-only scorches this cut.',
