    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Grill baby backs are shorter than the smoker 2-2-1 method for a reason — a grill runs hotter than a smoker even in the indirect zone, so the same 6-hour method turns the rack to shredded mush. Stop when the bend test bows deep and cracks the surface, not at a fixed time; a rack pulled at 90 minutes because the recipe said so still eats tough, and a rack held for 3 hours because you were busy comes off in strings.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F safe minimum for pork; 200–203°F collagen-render target for tender ribs per Meathead Goldwyn\'s rib-doneness guide on AmazingRibs.com. Grill (as opposed to smoker) two-zone method and 300°F indirect time range from the Weber Grilling Guide baby-back-ribs chart and Steven Raichlen\'s Project Smoke grill-baby-backs technique.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Ribs are dominated by collagen-rich connective tissue between the bones, and collagen does not soften on the same schedule as muscle protein: its structure needs sustained time well above body temperature to break down into gelatin, which is why the useful target here is 200-203°F rather than the safe-minimum number crossed early in the cook. That gelatin conversion happens gradually as heat and time accumulate, rather than flipping at one sharp threshold the way muscle-fiber coagulation does, which is part of why doneness gets judged by feel instead of a single number. The membrane stripped from the bone side matters separately: it is dense and largely impermeable, so removing it lets conduction and rendered fat move through the underside of the rack as freely as the meaty top.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'smoker-bbq-baby-back-ribs',
    keywords: ['how long to grill baby back ribs', 'baby back ribs on the grill', 'grill ribs indirect', 'baby back ribs bend test', 'grill baby back ribs temperature'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to grill baby back ribs: two-zone indirect at 300°F, 1.5–2 hours to the bend test. Why grill ribs finish faster than the 2-2-1 smoker method.',
