    proTip: 'All-beef dogs have real collagen casings and will split lengthwise if the fire is too hot — a scorched, blown-out dog dumps its fat and eats dry. Medium-high direct with quarter-turns beats the hottest zone of the grill, and the diagonal scoring is not decoration: the cuts give the casing somewhere to release pressure so it never bursts down the middle.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS Hot Dogs and Food Safety fact sheet — pre-cooked hot dogs are reheated to steaming (~140°F) for the general population, with 165°F reserved for pregnant, immunocompromised, or elderly consumers per the same fact sheet. Grill time and quarter-turn technique from the Weber Grilling Guide hot-dog chart and Meathead Goldwyn\'s grilled hot dog piece on AmazingRibs.com.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Because the meat inside is already a fully cooked, finely emulsified paste of fat, water, and protein rather than intact muscle fiber, heat moves through it almost like a liquid, with no fiber orientation to slow conduction, so the interior tracks a 425°F grate surface far faster than a raw cut of similar diameter would. That speed is also why the target here is a reheating number rather than a coagulation number: the protein network was already denatured into a stable gel during the original manufacturing cook, so 140°F simply restores serving temperature rather than triggering any structural change on the grill. The rocker shape from scoring also increases how much curved surface is exposed to radiant heat at any one rotation, which is part of why it chars across more surface than an unscored dog in the same total time.',
