    donenessCue: 'A wooden spoon pressed into the apples meets no resistance and the flesh collapses into itself; the water in the pot has turned to a rose-tinted syrup as the peels bleed pigment; a raw taste is gone and the aroma is bright rather than raw-apple sharp.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Leave the peels on for the whole cook and blend at the end with an immersion blender right in the pot — the peels are where nearly all the pigment and most of the pectin live, so pulling them out first gives you pale, watery applesauce that needs sugar to taste like anything. Blended peel is invisible in the finished sauce.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning "Applesauce" (fresh apple cook to full breakdown at simmer is 20–30 min stovetop, 5–6 hrs slow-cooker LOW; peels contain most of the pectin and pigment); National Center for Home Food Preservation applesauce reference; Crock-Pot 6-Qt recipe archive "Homemade Applesauce" (LOW 5–6 hrs); no probe target — doneness is a spoon-collapse test typical for fruit purées, so internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Softening here is a pectin story rather than a protein one: the middle lamella cementing apple cells together is built from pectin that gradually hydrolyzes under sustained heat and the mild acidity of the lemon juice, and once enough of those bonds break, the cells separate from each other and the fruit collapses rather than merely going soft at the surface. Because a slow cooker\'s liquid environment never approaches the dry, radiant heat that drives browning, none of these sugars ever caramelize the way they would in a hot pan; the rosy tint instead comes from pigment compounds in the peel slowly leaching into the liquid.',
    keywords: ['how long to slow cook applesauce', 'crockpot applesauce from fresh apples', 'slow cooker cinnamon applesauce time', 'homemade applesauce slow cooker', 'no peel applesauce crockpot'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook applesauce in the slow cooker: 5–6 hours on LOW with the peels on. Why the peels stay in the pot the whole time.',
