    technicalDeepDive: 'A brisket flat is nearly all lean muscle threaded with connective tissue and carries comparatively little marbling, so it depends almost entirely on that tissue liquefying for moistness rather than on rendered fat. Searing before the braise locks a Maillard crust onto the surface while the meat is still dry, since the slow cooker itself never climbs into the 280-330°F range needed to brown anything once the lid is on. Sitting fat-cap-up over the onions lets that thin fat layer render slowly downward by gravity, basting the lean muscle from within as the tough fibrous network breaks down over nine to ten hours.',
