    verificationBasis: 'USA Dry Bean Council "Slow Cooker Cooking Dry Beans" (unsoaked black beans: LOW 7–9 hrs to fully tender); USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning legume prep reference (soaking is not required for pressure-cooked or long-simmered beans if the cook is long enough); USDA FoodData Central notes on pectin firming under low pH and high sodium; Crock-Pot 6-Qt recipe archive "Slow-Cooker Black Beans" (LOW 8–9 hrs from dry). No probe target — doneness is a smash-against-the-pot test standard for legumes across the dataset, so internalTempTargetF is intentionally omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Dry beans don\'t strictly need pre-soaking here because the eight-to-nine-hour hold gives water enough time to diffuse fully through the seed coat on its own, gradually swelling and gelatinizing the starch at the bean\'s core rather than requiring a separate soak first. Holding the pot at a gentle sub-boil rather than a rolling boil matters too: rapid boiling agitates beans hard enough to split loosened skins, while steady low heat lets the pectin in the cell walls break down evenly so the interior turns creamy while the skin stays intact. The sofrito\'s oil-sautéed aromatics dissolve into the broth rather than the beans, since their fat-soluble flavor compounds need a fat carrier to penetrate the mostly-water matrix.',
    keywords: ['how long to slow cook black beans', 'crockpot black beans from dry', 'slow cooker black beans unsoaked', 'black beans low all day', 'no soak black beans slow cooker'],
