    technicalDeepDive: 'A five-to-six-pound bone-in shoulder holds enough mass that salt needs real time to work: sodium ions diffuse into muscle slowly, so the extended length of this cook, roughly double a standard pulled-pork timeline, gives the rubbed salt a long path to reach the core instead of staying concentrated in the outer inch. Liquid smoke behaves the same way, its flavor compounds gradually migrating inward through the fibers over those same hours rather than sitting on the surface. Bone conducts heat more slowly than the surrounding muscle, so the center near the bone is typically the last part of the roast to fully finish converting its connective tissue, which is part of why a bone-in cut like this needs more time than a boneless one of similar weight.',
