    cutOrPrep: '1.25–1.5 lb outside skirt steak in one piece (about 1/2-inch thick), marinated 2–4 hours in oil, garlic, lime and salt, patted dry before cooking',
    state: 'refrigerated',
    tempF: 550,
    tempC: 288,
    tempFormatted: 'Screaming hot (550°F+ surface) — pan smoking before the steak goes down',
    timeMinMinutes: 3,
    timeMaxMinutes: 5,
    timeFormatted: '3–5 mins (90 seconds per side, no second flip)',
    flipAtMinutes: 2,
    internalTempTargetF: 125,
    internalTempTargetFormatted: '125°F (pull) → 130–135°F medium-rare after 5-min rest',
    restMinutes: 5,
    donenessCue: 'The exterior is a dark, blistered crust with visibly charred edges where the loose fringes hang off the muscle; the steak has firmed to a springy medium-rare and the marinade sugars have caramelised almost to burnt in patches.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Skirt is thin and unforgiving — the pan has to be smoking hot before the steak touches it or you end up braising the meat in its own juice and get grey, chewy skirt. And you always slice it across the visible long grain into 1/4-inch strips; sliced with the grain, even a perfectly cooked skirt eats like a rubber band.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F whole-cut beef minimum internal temperature with 3-min rest; medium-rare pull of 125°F with 5-min rest documented per Serious Eats skirt steak guide (thin cuts carryover slower than thick ones, ~5–10°F rise) and ThermoWorks technique reference. Grain direction and 1/4-inch cross-cut per J. Kenji López-Alt "The Food Lab" chapter on skirt and hanger steaks.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Skirt is thin enough that the conduction path from surface to center is short, so the whole cut heats through fast once it\'s on the pan, which is why the surface needs to be screaming hot, since there\'s only a narrow window to build a crust before that thin geometry lets the center overshoot into well-done. The lime in the marinade does chemical work before any heat is involved, denaturing surface proteins through acidity rather than temperature, which is part of why the exterior firms and takes on color even during the marinating step. The oil in the marinade also helps once the steak hits the pan, filling microscopic gaps between meat and iron that conduction otherwise has to bridge through trapped air.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook skirt steak cast iron', 'skirt steak internal temp', 'cast iron skirt steak time', 'skirt steak against the grain', 'marinated skirt steak skillet'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook skirt steak in cast iron: 3–5 minutes total in a screaming-hot pan to a 125°F pull. Why the grain direction matters more than the cook.',
