    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'The beer poach is not for flavor — a strong beer actually turns the casings bitter — it is for even cooking. Fresh bratwurst have a thick pork mixture that seizes if you sear the whole cook, giving you burnt casings around raw pink centres. Sear first for colour, simmer in cheap lager to finish the cook gently, then sear briefly again to re-crisp the wet casings; that final crisp is the whole point of doing it in cast iron instead of just boiling them.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 160°F minimum internal temperature for fresh (raw) pork and beef sausage links — no rest allowance, must hit temperature during the cook. Sear-poach-sear method per Johnsonville "Bratwurst Cooking Instructions" (official guide) and Serious Eats "The Best Way to Cook Brats" reference; beer-braise volume calibrated to 12 oz per five links so the liquid covers the bottom half of the sausages only, preventing the casings from splitting.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The natural casing is a collagen membrane holding the pork mixture\'s moisture and rendering fat under pressure as it heats, and searing straight through at high heat would build that pressure faster than the casing can stretch, which is how links split. Simmering in beer switches the heat-transfer mode from direct conductive contact to convection through a surrounding liquid, so the interior climbs to a safe temperature evenly instead of one side building pressure faster than the rest. Because the filling is ground and mixed like any emulsified sausage, it carries the same food-safety logic as ground beef: bacteria gets distributed through the mixture, so the whole link has to reach temperature, not just the surface. The final sear then only has to push a wet, pale casing back past the Maillard threshold.',
