    technicalDeepDive: 'Cooking the ribeye through a low oven lets heat migrate to the center by gentle convection, keeping the surface-to-center gradient small on the way to 115°F, unlike the wide gradient a direct hot-pan sear produces, which leaves a grey overcooked band under the crust. Because the roast dry-brines and then sits uncovered in the oven\'s dry air, its surface reaches the pan already dehydrated, so conducted heat goes straight into browning instead of boiling off surface water first. The bone conducts more slowly than muscle, so the long, gentle oven stage gives meat near the bone time to catch up before the brief hard sear locks the crust in.',
