    technicalDeepDive: 'Cheesecake batter is essentially a baked custard: egg proteins unfold and link into a gel network as they coagulate, trapping the cream cheese\'s fat and moisture inside that lattice. Cooking pot-in-pot over simmering water delivers gentle, even steam heat rather than an oven\'s direct radiant heat, so the outer batter never overshoots into temperatures that curdle egg protein and crack a conventionally baked cheesecake. Because heat conducts inward through the batter from the pan\'s edges, the center naturally lags behind and is still mid-coagulation when the timer ends. The hour-long rest lets that lag close out on its own, as retained heat keeps gently setting the center after the pot opens.',
