    technicalDeepDive: 'A dry pinto bean starts with almost no free water in its cotyledon, so the first job of a long cycle is hydration, not just heating, since starch granules cannot gelatinize until water has diffused all the way into them. Raising the chamber to roughly 239°F under pressure speeds that diffusion far beyond what atmospheric simmering achieves in the same span, which is why sealed pressure can finish an unsoaked bean in under an hour. Skipping the soak means the bean\'s dense structure has no head start, so much of the cycle goes to driving water inward before the starch can swell and the cell walls can give way to mashing.',
