    technicalDeepDive: 'Apple flesh is mostly water held together by pectin in the walls between cells; heat doesn\'t really cook the fruit so much as dissolve that pectin, letting cells slip apart from one another. Because the chunks are small and already saturated with moisture, steam near 239°F conducts through them in minutes and pushes the pectin past its softening point, with no dense muscle fiber or starch granule slowing that diffusion the way there is in a roast or a dry bean. Five minutes collapses the outer layer of each chunk into pulp while a firmer core survives, giving pressure-cooked applesauce its rustic texture instead of a uniform puree.',
