    restMinutes: 3,
    donenessCue: 'A deep uniform mahogany crust on both faces (not gray or spotty); butter foam has stopped bubbling and turned nut-brown around the edges; probe at the thickest center reads 143°F–145°F; juices run clear-blush, not red.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'The 30-minute wet brine is non-negotiable for boneless chops — modern lean pork loses 30% of its moisture at 145°F without it, giving you the chalky-dry chops that give pork its bad reputation. Pull the chop from the pan at 140°F, not 145°F: it will climb 5°F during the mandatory 3-minute rest, landing you exactly on the USDA safe minimum with maximum juice retention. Boneless is faster than bone-in — the bone acts as heat sink and adds 3–4 minutes to a 1-inch chop.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Fresh Pork from Farm to Table" 145°F safe minimum + 3-min rest (revised 2011 down from 160°F); National Pork Board pan-seared chop protocol; Cook\'s Illustrated "Best Wet Brine for Pork Chops" (30 min in 5% salt + 3% sugar solution); Lodge Cast Iron 12-inch skillet high-heat searing guide; ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A wet brine changes lean pork through osmosis: dissolved salt raises the ionic strength inside the muscle fibers enough to loosen tightly packed myosin, so the meat holds more water even as heat drives coagulation forward. At 425 to 450°F the pan pushes the chop\'s exterior through the Maillard range within minutes, well before the center nears the 145°F USDA threshold for whole cuts, a threshold lower than ground meat\'s because pathogens on an intact muscle stay confined to the surface. The final butter baste layers convective heat from moving hot fat atop the pan\'s own conduction, and the milk solids browning in that butter add caramelized notes to the crust in the closing minutes.',
