    donenessCue: 'No pink or red meat visible anywhere in the crumble (unlike whole steak, medium-rare is not safe for ground beef); rendered fat has pooled and clarified; browned bits (fond) cling to the pan bottom; a probe through the thickest crumble cluster reads 160°F.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Do not stir for the first 3 minutes — sit-and-brown builds the Maillard crust that gives ground beef its meaty depth. Constant stirring steams the meat gray and washes out the fond. Drain rendered fat AFTER the browning is complete, not before; fat carries fat-soluble flavor compounds you need for the initial sear. For chili, leave 1 tbsp back to bloom the spices in; for taco filling, drain all but a slick and add taco seasoning + 1/2 cup water directly to the pan.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Ground Beef and Food Safety" 160°F safe minimum for all ground meat (dispersed-surface-bacteria rule); Cook\'s Illustrated "The Best Way to Brown Ground Beef" (dry preheated skillet, undisturbed initial sear); Serious Eats / J. Kenji López-Alt Maillard-reaction browning method; Lodge Cast Iron 12-inch skillet high-heat cooking guide; ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Grinding multiplies beef\'s surface area many times over, which is why the USDA sets its safe minimum at 160°F instead of the 145°F used for whole muscle: bacteria once confined to the exterior get distributed throughout the meat during grinding, so the whole mass must reach that higher mark. Leaving the beef undisturbed on a 400 to 425°F dry pan for the first three minutes matters because each chunk is still shedding surface moisture, and that moisture must evaporate before the local temperature can climb past 212°F into the Maillard range; stirring early just recirculates steam and stalls browning. As fat renders out it pools and conducts heat efficiently into the meat still cooking around it, accelerating the browning once it starts.',
