    proTip: 'Do not thaw or oil these — Ore-Ida par-fries the potatoes in oil before freezing, and adding a second coat of oil on top of the pan just makes them greasy and steam-wet instead of crisp. The single flip at the 12-minute mark is what separates the "burned top, pale bottom" home-oven outcome from the fast-food-diner outcome; skip it and one side stays raw-tasting.',
    verificationBasis: 'Ore-Ida Golden Crinkles retail bag directions (H.J. Heinz Company) instruct: "Preheat oven to 425°F. Spread frozen fries in a single layer on a 15x10-inch baking sheet. Bake 22 to 24 minutes." The 20-minute low bound accounts for a hotter convection oven or a partial bag; the 12-minute flip is called out on the bag as "stir once during baking for even browning."',
    technicalDeepDive: 'These fries are already par-fried in oil at the factory, so the fat needed for crisping is already distributed through the starch and the pan\'s job is purely to drive off the ice and surface frost left from freezing before any browning can start. That ice has to absorb real energy just to become water before the surface can pass 212°F and keep climbing toward the range where Maillard browning begins, which is why the crinkle ridges — thinner and offering more edge per gram than a straight cut — color first while the denser cores are still catching up. Flipping at 12 minutes swaps which ridges face the hot aluminum, since a ridge sitting in a trough stays shielded from direct pan contact until it\'s rotated into it.',
