    technicalDeepDive: 'The fat-and-starch coating on each nugget already underwent a par-fry step in processing, so it isn\'t raw dough clinging to raw meat — it\'s a semi-cooked matrix that the sheet pan\'s job is to dry out and press back into a crisp shell. Breading is a poor conductor, so it buffers the meat from the hot pan air, which is part of why the batch still needs the fuller 11 to 13 minutes even though the interior was fully cooked before it was ever frozen. Flipping at seven minutes matters more here than with raw poultry, because it\'s the breading\'s contact with the aluminum, not the meat\'s temperature climb, that decides whether both faces end up equally fractured and crisp — the 165°F reheat floor is really just confirming the core caught back up to the crust.',
