    technicalDeepDive: 'These rings arrive already par-fried, so the beer batter\'s starch has already gelatinized into a lace-like shell holding a thin layer of trapped oil that only needs re-liquefying, not cooking from scratch. High-velocity convection strips the humid air clinging to that frozen shell almost immediately, letting the surface dehydrate and re-brown through the Maillard reaction well before the onion\'s own internal moisture has time to migrate outward and soften it. Because the shell is thin and structurally set from its first fry, it needs only eight to ten minutes at 400°F to reheat through and re-crisp, far less time than a raw-battered ring would need just to cook its coating from a liquid state.',
