    technicalDeepDive: 'Cutting sirloin into 1-inch cubes multiplies exposed surface relative to volume, which matters because browning only happens where the meat\'s surface itself reaches Maillard range while the geometric center lags well behind. Sirloin carries comparatively little intramuscular collagen next to a braising cut, so there\'s no need for a long, low cook to convert connective tissue into gelatin; the entire goal here is denaturing and lightly coagulating myofibrillar protein before it seizes and expels its own moisture. Pulling at 135°F leaves headroom for carryover, since heat stored in the browned crust keeps conducting inward toward the cube\'s center for a few more degrees during the rest.',
