    technicalDeepDive: 'Cheese sauces are dairy emulsions held together by casein proteins and milk fat, both heat-sensitive in a way lean meat isn\'t: push them past a gentle simmer and casein over-coagulates while the fat separates out, which is why HIGH scorches this dish and LOW is the only setting listed. Cream cheese\'s stabilizers help hold that emulsion together through the two-plus-hour cook, while the uncooked macaroni gelatinizes its starch by slowly absorbing milk and cream directly instead of boiling water, which is why so much liquid is needed relative to the pasta volume. Because the mixture never nears the Maillard range, any surface browning is dehydration and light caramelization of milk sugars at the exposed edge, not a true crust.',
