    technicalDeepDive: 'Grinding beef mechanically redistributes any surface bacteria throughout the meat rather than leaving it on the exterior, which is why ground chuck needs to reach a uniform 165°F internally rather than relying on the surface searing that protects a whole roast. That browning has to happen on the stovetop before the meat goes into the crock, since the Maillard reaction only kicks in above roughly 280°F, and once the beef is submerged in tomatoes and bean liquid, the aqueous environment caps the slow cooker at essentially a bare simmer. Crushed tomatoes also bring enough acidity to slow how quickly the kidney beans\' pectin breaks down, which is part of why the six-to-eight-hour hold matters even though the meat itself reaches safe temperature much earlier.',
