    technicalDeepDive: 'Unlike a brisket flat, chuck carries meaningful intramuscular marbling woven between its fibers, so as those fibers heat and tighten the way cooked muscle protein does, fat rendering from within helps keep the meat moist even before the dense collagen network has fully broken down. Submerging the roast in beef broth means heat reaches it mainly by convection through the liquid, gentler and more even across the surface than dry oven heat at the same endpoint. Nine hours on Low gives collagen in this well-worked shoulder muscle room to dissolve into gelatin by the time the center hits 203°F, well past where muscle fibers alone would have turned dry and stringy. Worcestershire\'s fermented base also seasons the meat from the outside in as broth slowly penetrates it.',
