    technicalDeepDive: 'Introducing milk to the meat before the tomatoes matters chemically, not just for flavor: milk\'s proteins and fat would curdle and break if they hit the tomatoes\' acidity directly, so cooking the dairy into the meat first, while the mixture is still closer to neutral pH, lets it bind smoothly before any acid arrives. Blending pork into the beef raises the fat content, and that fat slowly renders and disperses into the sauce over the long simmer rather than pooling on top, which is what gives finished Bolognese its silky rather than greasy mouthfeel. Ground meat also has an enormous surface-area-to-volume ratio compared to a whole roast, so it reaches safe temperature almost immediately, meaning the six-to-eight-hour hold at 170°F internal is really about letting the mirepoix break down and the sauce concentrate rather than about finishing the meat itself.',
