    technicalDeepDive: 'Vinegar added at the start lowers the liquid\'s pH just enough to help pull calcium and minerals out of the bone matrix while assisting the slow hydrolysis of collagen into gelatin. Cartilage-rich cuts like knuckles carry an especially dense collagen network, which is why broth needs eighteen to twenty-four hours at a bare simmer rather than the shorter hold a solid piece of meat would need. Keeping the liquid at a gentle simmer rather than a rolling boil also matters for clarity: vigorous boiling emulsifies rendered fat directly into the water and clouds it, while the slow cooker\'s steady sub-boil heat lets fat separate out cleanly instead. The broth\'s ability to set into a gel once chilled is a direct readout of how much collagen actually converted into dissolved gelatin over that long hold.',
