    technicalDeepDive: 'The masseter and other jaw muscles that become beef cheeks work continuously through an animal\'s life chewing and grinding, giving them a tightly bundled fiber structure unlike a rarely-used cut such as tenderloin. What makes a well-cooked cheek taste succulent isn\'t intramuscular fat, which this cut has relatively little of, but dissolved gelatin, a protein that can hold many times its own weight in water and keeps the meat moist even though it looks lean going in. Red wine\'s tannins bind to proteins on the meat\'s surface during the initial sear, deepening the fond that later dissolves back into the braising liquid as the cook progresses. As gelatin accumulates in that liquid over the eight-to-nine-hour hold, it also thickens the sauce itself into something glossy, essentially building its own body without any added flour or cornstarch.',
